        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.345,
            0.835,
            0.364
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.8: Let us make a model"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.49,
            0.367,
            0.89,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "Make your own centrifuge with a cardboard disc and thick thread. You will be able to see how the heavier particles move outwards. It is a fun and hands-on way to understand the science behind separation. Which mixture would you like to separate using this mini centrifuge?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.492,
            0.65,
            0.511
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "B. Coagulation"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.49,
            0.513,
            0.89,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "Powdered alum (fitkari) can be added to a beaker containing muddy water (Fig. 5.21a). Alum is a white crystalline chemical substance often used in water purification. The alum causes the fine suspended particles to clump together. This process is called **coagulation** and the alum is said to act as a **coagulant** (Fig. 5.21b). These larger clumps thus formed, settle by gravity (sedimentation), as shown in Fig. 5.21c and can be separated from water by decantation or filtration."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.718,
            0.888,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "Can you think of any other coagulation processes used in everyday life?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.756,
            0.889,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "The formation of cheese (paneer) from milk also involves coagulation, in which acid (lemon juice or vinegar) is used as a coagulant. It causes the coagulation of milk proteins, forming cheese."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.814,
            0.378,
            0.833
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.4.4 Colloids"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.836,
            0.889,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "If the components of blood can be separated by centrifugation and the blood coagulates, is it a suspension? However, we cannot see blood cells with the naked eye. Is it a solution?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.891,
            0.889,
            0.927
          ],
          "content": "Blood is neither a solution nor a true suspension. It is a **colloid**. Some other examples of colloids are milk, tomato sauce and ice cream."
        },
        {
